00:01:39.177 --> 00:01:44.157
E-PAM systems, according to Simply Wall Street, reported an earnings beat paired with a new

00:01:44.157 --> 00:01:49.117
buyback plan, and the live question is whether the post-reaction price-to-earnings ratio

00:01:49.117 --> 00:01:51.557
still leaves room against the company's growth path.

00:01:51.557 --> 00:01:56.437
Yeah, valuations screening after earnings beats is the filter that answers that question.

00:01:56.837 --> 00:02:00.817
You take reported earnings, any buyback announcement, and the forward multiple.

00:02:01.357 --> 00:02:06.017
Then you ask whether the name is cheap relative to its own trajectory, rather than whether

00:02:06.017 --> 00:02:07.197
the headline looked loud.

00:02:07.837 --> 00:02:10.257
The working rule is simple enough to run after dinner.

00:02:10.897 --> 00:02:15.217
Screen the post earnings price to earnings ratio, compare it with the five-year average,

00:02:15.597 --> 00:02:19.557
and require free cash flow yield above 4% before you add a share.

00:02:19.557 --> 00:02:24.017
Think of it like reading the unit price on two cereal boxes after a coupon.

00:02:24.597 --> 00:02:30.297
The coupon is the beat and the buyback and the unit price is that forward multiple versus the five-year average,

00:02:30.717 --> 00:02:33.757
which is the only number that tells you whether the discount is real.

00:02:34.417 --> 00:02:40.917
You pull the latest quarterly earnings per share and shares outstanding data in your brokerage research tool or on Yahoo Finance.

00:02:41.397 --> 00:02:45.877
Divide market cap by that figure and you get a forward multiple you can actually compare.

00:02:45.877 --> 00:02:51.357
The key step is exporting that quarterly data into a simple spreadsheet so the same formula

00:02:51.357 --> 00:02:55.877
hits every candidate the same way, which is how you stop a favorite name from getting

00:02:55.877 --> 00:02:57.477
a softer test than a stranger.

00:02:58.217 --> 00:03:02.837
Apply the filter only to names already on your watch list, because a clean screen on a

00:03:02.837 --> 00:03:04.837
random beat is just a dressed up chase.

00:03:05.477 --> 00:03:09.917
The same approach worked when similar software names traded back to their historical multiples

00:03:09.917 --> 00:03:15.217
within four weeks after beats, and it failed when sector rotation pulled capital into larger

00:03:15.217 --> 00:03:20.257
appears instead of the name that had just reported. Canadian investors can run that screen

00:03:20.257 --> 00:03:26.157
inside a tax-free savings account, or TFSA, without triggering an immediate tax event on any

00:03:26.157 --> 00:03:30.777
rebalancing that follows, which is the practical reason to do the homework before the next

00:03:30.777 --> 00:03:37.219
open rather than during it. Advanced micro-devices is back on the simulated pad, this time as

