00:10:10.689 --> 00:10:12.089
Here is how I would think about it.

00:10:12.329 --> 00:10:16.989
A payroll beat with unemployment already lower lifts short-term yields first, and that

00:10:16.989 --> 00:10:21.869
keeps defensive sectors bid until the actual policy decision, so the headline that looks

00:10:21.869 --> 00:10:25.209
like growth fuel can arrive as a discount rate headwind instead.

00:10:25.209 --> 00:10:29.929
head. Professionals check the two-year treasury yield move within 30 minutes of the release

00:10:29.929 --> 00:10:34.749
and compare it with the prior three prints before they reallocate a dollar. The concrete

00:10:34.749 --> 00:10:39.849
misconception is assuming every job's beat automatically favors growth stocks. A print

00:10:39.849 --> 00:10:44.649
that exceeds forecasts can still produce mixed-sector moves because a tighter labor market signals

00:10:44.649 --> 00:10:50.809
tighter policy ahead rather than broad acceleration. The fixes to wait for the yield curve response,

00:10:50.809 --> 00:10:56.149
months, then scan sector ETFs for relative strength only after that data settles, which

00:10:56.149 --> 00:11:00.489
is how you avoid entries that look attractive on the headline and then eat rising discount

00:11:00.489 --> 00:11:01.289
rates for breakfast.

00:11:02.069 --> 00:11:04.449
Remember, wait for the yield before you rotate.

